## DESCRIPTION
##   Working with Vectors
## ENDDESCRIPTION

## KEYWORDS('Vector', 'Length')
## Tagged by nhamblet

## DBsubject('Calculus')
## DBchapter('Vectors and the Geometry of Space')
## DBsection('Vectors')
## Date('8/23/07')
## Author('')
## Institution('Union College')
## TitleText1('Calculus')
## EditionText1('7')
## AuthorText1('Anton')
## Section1('12.2')
## Problem1('15')
## TitleText2('Calculus: Early Transcendentals')
## EditionText2('1')
## AuthorText2('Rogawski')
## Section2('12.2')
## Problem2('20')

DOCUMENT();    # This should be the first executable line in the problem.

loadMacros(
    "PGstandard.pl",           'PGML.pl',
    "contextLimitedVector.pl", "parserVectorUtils.pl",
    "PGcourse.pl"
);

Context("Vector");

# the vectors
$U = non_zero_vector3D();
$V = non_zero_vector3D();
$W = non_zero_vector3D();

# some coefficients
($a, $b, $c, $d) = (random(2, 8), random(2, 8), random(2, 8), random(2, 8));

$ans_1 = norm($U) + norm($V);
$ans_2 = norm(-$a * $U) + $b * norm($V);
$ans_3 = norm($c * $U - $d * ($V + $W));
$ans_4 = $W / norm($W);
$ans_5 = norm($ans_4);

#  Don't allow norm or ||v|| in student answers
Context()->functions->undefine('norm');
Context()
    ->lists->set(
        AbsoluteValue => { class => 'LimitedVector::List::AbsoluteValue' });

BEGIN_PGML
Suppose [`\vec{u}=[$U], \vec{v}=[$V]`] and [`\vec{w} = [$W]`]. 

Compute the following values:

a) [`||\vec{u}||+||\vec{v}||=`] [_]{$ans_1}

b) [`||-[$a]\vec{u}|| + [$b]||\vec{v}||`] = [_]{$ans_2}

c) [`||[$c]\vec{u} - [$d]\vec{v} + \vec{w}||`] = [_]{$ans_3}

d) [`` \frac{1}{||\vec{w}||}\vec{w} ``] = [_]{$ans_4}

e) [`` \left|\left|\frac{1}{||\vec{w}||}\vec{w}\right|\right|``] = [_]{$ans_5}

END_PGML

# reset the context so norm can be used.
Context('Vector');

$v3 = $c * $U - $d * $V + $W;
BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION
First, note that 
[```\begin{aligned}
    ||\vec{u}|| & = \sqrt{([$U->{data}[0]])^2+([$U->{data}[1]])^2+([$U->{data}[2]])^2}
    = \sqrt{[@ norm($U)**2 @]} \\
    ||\vec{v} & =  \sqrt{([$V->{data}[0]])^2+([$V->{data}[1]])^2+([$V->{data}[2]])^2}
    = \sqrt{[@ norm($V)**2 @]} \\
    ||\vec{w}|| & =  \sqrt{([$W->{data}[0]])^2+([$W->{data}[1]])^2+([$W->{data}[2]])^2}
    = \sqrt{[@ norm($V)**2 @]} \\
    \end{aligned}
```]

a) [`` ||\vec{u}||+||\vec{v}|| = \sqrt{[@ norm($U)**2 @]} + \sqrt{[@ norm($V)**2 @]} ``]

b) [`` ||-[$a]\vec{u}||+[$b]||\vec{v}|| = [$a]\sqrt{[@ norm($U)**2 @]} + [$b] \sqrt{[@ norm($V)**2 @]} ``]

c) [`\;`]
[``` ||[$c]\vec{u} - [$d]\vec{v} + \vec{w}|| = ||[$v3]||
= \sqrt{[@ norm($v3)**2 @]}```]

d) [`` \frac{1}{||\vec{w}||}\vec{w} = \frac{1}{\sqrt{[@ norm($W)**2 @]}} \left([$W]\right) = [$ans_4]``]

e) One can find the norm of this or realize that the vector in d) is a unit vector, so the norm is [`1`].
END_PGML_SOLUTION

ENDDOCUMENT();
